1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,950
Lesson 6: the model tools

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:14,591
YA-NEC course, lesson 6: the model tools. We start from an example: the 20 m dipole at 10 m over average ground.

3
00:00:14,641 --> 00:00:21,932
74.2 + j10.7 ohm, 7.11 dBi at 29.5 degrees.

4
00:00:21,982 --> 00:00:29,207
The Geometry bar works on the whole antenna or on the selected wires. 'Move': Z = 5 m.

5
00:00:29,257 --> 00:00:39,341
The preview shows the antenna in its new place, and the box lists what changes in the wire table. Units are accepted too: 10cm, 6in, 2ft.

6
00:00:39,391 --> 00:00:46,474
'Apply move'.

7
00:00:46,524 --> 00:01:01,342
Now at 15 m: 74.2 + j30.1 ohm, 7.52 dBi, and the main lobe drops from 29.5 to 19.6 degrees. Sources and loads follow the wires by themselves.

8
00:01:01,392 --> 00:01:08,009
Quick commands: 'horizontal <-> vertical' turns the dipole upright around its centre.

9
00:01:08,059 --> 00:01:20,186
Vertical dipole, bottom end at 9.8 m: 76.5 + j22.7 ohm, 2.01 dBi. Average ground eats much more of a vertical antenna.

10
00:01:20,236 --> 00:01:28,316
'Undo' twice: back to the starting dipole. Every geometry command can be undone and redone.

11
00:01:28,366 --> 00:01:32,661
74.2 + j10.7 ohm again.

12
00:01:32,711 --> 00:01:40,703
Now a classic mistake: only 3 segments on the 10.4 m wire.

13
00:01:40,753 --> 00:01:47,607
The result still looks reasonable - 75.4 + j7.3 ohm - and that is exactly the danger.

14
00:01:47,657 --> 00:01:51,667
The Warnings tab says why.

15
00:01:51,717 --> 00:02:02,870
Segments longer than lambda/10 (2.11 m): 3.47 m. The NEC-2 guide advises against it; EZNEC flags it as 'segment length too long'.

16
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:09,996
'Check...' runs the data checks that need no calculation.

17
00:02:10,046 --> 00:02:17,205
42 checks, all fine: the data are valid. Segment length is the job of the next tool.

18
00:02:17,255 --> 00:02:26,539
'Segmentation...': segment length, segment/radius ratio, equal segments around sources and lines (rules from EZNEC and ARRL).

19
00:02:26,589 --> 00:02:36,549
Wire 1: segment too long, 3.47 m against a minimum of 2.09 m. Target level: prudent, lambda/20.

20
00:02:36,599 --> 00:02:42,613
'Preview' computes before and after without touching the model.

21
00:02:42,663 --> 00:02:47,680
From 3 to 11 segments, odd so the source stays in the centre.

22
00:02:47,730 --> 00:02:54,743
'Apply and recalculate'.

23
00:02:54,793 --> 00:03:02,744
Fixed: no warnings left. With a long model, this window can also open by itself before every calculation.

24
00:03:02,794 --> 00:03:18,950
New in 1.0.11: the fill handle, as in Excel. Let's open an example with several wires: the DK7ZB 7-element EME Yagi.

25
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:25,250
In the wire table we write 4 mm in the reflector radius.

26
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:34,717
The active cell has a small blue square in the bottom right corner: drag it down over the other rows, which turn light blue.

27
00:03:34,767 --> 00:03:43,183
On release all the cells take the same value (variables and formulas too) and the model is recalculated at once.

28
00:03:43,233 --> 00:03:50,983
Ctrl+Z right after puts back the previous values: the fill is in the Undo history of the Geometry bar.

29
00:03:51,033 --> 00:04:01,066
The handle is also in the Sources, Loads and Lines tables. With several rows selected (Shift + arrows) the block is repeated; the Tag column has no handle.

30
00:04:01,116 --> 00:04:11,832
Move, rotate, scale, undo, check, segment: the model stays clean. Next lesson: simple HF beams from the calculators. 73 de IC8TEM
